(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[21] = {"n":21,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 30% · B2 50% · B2+ 20%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Neuroplasticity","p":["For much of the twentieth century, scientists believed that the adult brain was essentially fixed. According to this view, the brain developed rapidly in childhood, but once a person reached adulthood, its structure could not change significantly, and lost brain cells could never be replaced. Doctors therefore assumed that patients with serious brain injuries had little hope of significant recovery. Today, this idea has been largely abandoned. Research has shown that the brain remains capable of reorganizing itself throughout life, a property known as neuroplasticity.","Neuroplasticity operates at several levels. At the smallest scale, the connections between nerve cells, called synapses, become stronger when they are used repeatedly and weaker when they are neglected. This principle is often summarized in the phrase 'neurons that fire together wire together'. At a larger scale, entire regions of the brain can take on new functions. In people who are blind from birth, for example, areas normally used for vision may become involved in processing touch and sound, which may help to explain why many blind people read Braille with remarkable speed.","Some of the most striking evidence comes from studies of skilled professionals. In a well-known study, researchers scanned the brains of London taxi drivers, who must memorize thousands of streets before receiving a licence. The drivers had a larger hippocampus, a region involved in spatial memory, than people in a comparison group, and the difference was greater in those who had worked longer. Interestingly, the region appeared to shrink slightly after drivers retired. Similar changes have been observed in musicians, whose brain areas controlling finger movement and hearing tend to be more developed.","Neuroplasticity has important implications for medicine. After a stroke, which damages part of the brain by cutting off its blood supply, patients often lose the ability to move a limb or to speak. Rehabilitation therapies take advantage of plasticity by encouraging intensive, repetitive practice, so that healthy areas of the brain gradually take over the lost functions. One approach, known as constraint-induced therapy, involves restricting the use of a patient's healthy arm, forcing them to use the weaker one. Many patients recover abilities that were once thought to be permanently lost.","However, plasticity is not always beneficial. The same mechanisms that allow us to learn useful skills can also strengthen harmful habits. Chronic pain, for instance, may persist partly because the nervous system becomes increasingly sensitive to pain signals, and addiction involves changes in the brain's reward circuits that make certain behaviours difficult to stop. Moreover, plasticity tends to decline with age, which is one reason why children usually acquire new languages and skills more easily than adults.","Nevertheless, the discovery of neuroplasticity offers an encouraging message. Although learning may become more effortful as we grow older, the brain never entirely loses its capacity to change. Activities that challenge the mind, such as learning a language, playing an instrument or memorizing the Holy Quran, together with physical exercise, adequate sleep and social interaction, all appear to support a healthy and adaptable brain. In a very real sense, what we repeatedly do shapes who we become."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the history of brain surgery","the brain's ability to change throughout life","how taxi drivers learn routes","the main causes of strokes"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"Every paragraph discusses neuroplasticity.","h":"قدرة الدماغ على التغير."},{"n":2,"s":"What did scientists believe for much of the twentieth century?","o":["The brain is fully developed at birth.","Lost brain cells could easily be replaced.","The adult brain could not change significantly.","Children's brains change more slowly than adults'."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 describes the old view.","h":"الدماغ البالغ ثابت."},{"n":3,"s":"The word 'neglected' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to","o":["damaged by illness","used very often","connected together","not used or given attention"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Synapses weaken when they are not used.","h":"مُهمَل."},{"n":4,"s":"Why might many blind people read Braille quickly?","o":["Their fingers are more sensitive from birth.","Braille is simpler than printed text.","They read more books than sighted people.","Brain areas normally used for vision may help to process touch."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"مناطق البصر تعالج اللمس."},{"n":5,"s":"What does the change in the drivers' brains after retirement suggest?","o":["Taxi driving damages the brain.","Brain changes depend on continued use.","Older people cannot learn anything new.","The original study was inaccurate."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"The region shrank when it was no longer used.","h":"التغيير يعتمد على الاستخدام المستمر."},{"n":6,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'which' in 'After a stroke, which damages part of the brain' refers to","o":["the brain","the blood supply","a stroke","a limb"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"It is the stroke that damages the brain.","h":"تعود على السكتة."},{"n":7,"s":"Why does constraint-induced therapy restrict the healthy arm?","o":["to protect the healthy arm from injury","to reduce the patient's pain","to force the patient to use the weaker arm","to give the brain a rest"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"لإجبار المريض على استخدام الذراع الضعيفة."},{"n":8,"s":"Why does the writer mention chronic pain and addiction?","o":["to explain how strokes happen","to describe new treatments for pain","to prove that adults learn faster than children","to show that plasticity can have harmful effects"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Paragraph 5 begins 'plasticity is not always beneficial'.","h":"إظهار الجانب الضار."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as supporting a healthy brain?","o":["physical exercise","avoiding all technology","adequate sleep","social interaction"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Technology is not mentioned.","h":"التقنية غير مذكورة."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's attitude in the final paragraph?","o":["worried and critical","neutral and uninterested","encouraging and optimistic","doubtful and confused"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"It offers 'an encouraging message'.","h":"متفائل ومشجع."}]},{"t":"Deciphering Linear B","p":["In 1900, the British archaeologist Arthur Evans began excavating the ancient palace of Knossos on the island of Crete. Among his many discoveries were thousands of clay tablets covered in an unknown script. Evans called it Linear B, to distinguish it from an older, related script, Linear A. For the next half-century, Linear B remained one of the great mysteries of archaeology. Nobody knew what language it recorded, and without a bilingual text, such as the Rosetta Stone that had helped scholars to read Egyptian hieroglyphs, there seemed to be no obvious way to begin.","Evans himself was convinced that the tablets recorded a language of the Minoans, the people of ancient Crete, and that it had nothing to do with Greek. Because of his enormous reputation, few scholars dared to challenge this view. He also published only a small number of the tablets, which made it difficult for others to study the script. Progress was therefore slow for several decades.","An important breakthrough came from Alice Kober, an American classicist who worked on the problem in the 1940s, largely in her spare time. Kober refused to guess which language lay behind the script. Instead, she analysed the signs themselves, recording every occurrence on thousands of handmade cards. She noticed that some words appeared in several slightly different forms, which suggested that the language used endings to show grammar, as Greek and Latin do. These groups of related words, later called 'Kober's triplets', provided valuable clues about how the signs related to one another. Sadly, Kober died in 1950, before the script was deciphered.","The final step was taken by Michael Ventris, a British architect who had been fascinated by Linear B since he heard Evans lecture as a schoolboy. Building on Kober's work, Ventris arranged the signs into a grid, grouping those that seemed to share the same consonant or the same vowel. Like Evans, he initially believed that the language was not Greek. In 1952, however, he tested the idea that certain words were the names of places on Crete, such as Knossos. When he applied the sound values suggested by these names, other words began to look surprisingly like an early form of Greek.","Ventris was at first reluctant to accept his own conclusion, but he soon found support from John Chadwick, a specialist in the history of the Greek language. Together, they published their decipherment in 1953. Shortly afterwards, a newly discovered tablet from the mainland Greek city of Pylos provided powerful confirmation: it contained pictures of three-legged pots alongside words that, using Ventris's system, could be read as the Greek words for those very objects.","The decipherment transformed our understanding of the ancient Mediterranean. It showed that Greek was being written several centuries earlier than had been believed, around 1400 BCE. The tablets themselves turned out to be mostly administrative records listing goods, workers, animals and taxes, rather than literature or history. Even so, they reveal a great deal about the economy and organization of Mycenaean society. The story of Linear B is also a reminder that great discoveries often depend on patient, unglamorous work, and on the willingness to abandon a theory when the evidence points elsewhere. Linear A, meanwhile, remains undeciphered to this day."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["How an Ancient Script Was Finally Read","The Life of Arthur Evans","Building Palaces in Ancient Crete","Why Linear A Is Easy to Understand"],"k":0,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage tells the story of the decipherment.","h":"قصة فك الرموز."},{"n":12,"s":"Why did there seem to be no obvious way to begin decoding Linear B?","o":["The tablets were too damaged to read.","Evans refused to show any tablets.","The script used only pictures.","There was no bilingual text to compare it with."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 mentions the lack of a text like the Rosetta Stone.","h":"لا يوجد نص ثنائي اللغة."},{"n":13,"s":"What can be inferred about Evans's influence?","o":["His opinion discouraged other scholars from considering Greek.","He encouraged scholars to challenge his ideas.","He was the first to decipher Linear A.","He worked closely with Alice Kober."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Few scholars dared to challenge his view.","h":"رأيه منع الآخرين."},{"n":14,"s":"What did Kober's analysis suggest about the language?","o":["It used word endings to show grammar.","It was definitely Greek.","Each sign represented a whole word.","It was written from right to left."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"استخدام اللواحق للقواعد."},{"n":15,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'those' in 'grouping those that seemed to share' refers to","o":["the vowels","the signs","the schoolboys","the lectures"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"Ventris grouped the signs.","h":"تعود على الرموز."},{"n":16,"s":"Which of these events happened LAST?","o":["A tablet from Pylos confirmed the decipherment.","Kober recorded signs on handmade cards.","Ventris tested the names of places.","Evans began excavating Knossos."],"k":0,"ty":"sequence","e":"The Pylos tablet came after the 1953 publication.","h":"لوح بيلوس هو الأخير."},{"n":17,"s":"Why does the writer mention the tablet from Pylos?","o":["to show how the decipherment was confirmed","to show that Ventris had made a mistake","to introduce the script Linear A","to describe Mycenaean art"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"It 'provided powerful confirmation'.","h":"إثبات صحة الحل."},{"n":18,"s":"According to the passage, what were most Linear B tablets?","o":["poems and stories","religious texts","administrative records","letters between kings"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 6 states this.","h":"سجلات إدارية."},{"n":19,"s":"The word 'unglamorous' in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to","o":["very expensive","not exciting or admired","extremely quick","widely reported"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Patient, ordinary work.","h":"غير لافت."},{"n":20,"s":"What lesson does the writer draw from the story?","o":["Famous scholars are always right.","Discoveries need patient work and a willingness to change one's mind.","Ancient scripts can never be fully understood.","Architects make better scientists than classicists."],"k":1,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"The last paragraph states this lesson.","h":"الصبر والمرونة."}]},{"t":"Carbon capture technology","p":["As the effects of climate change become more severe, governments and companies are searching for ways to reduce the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere. The most important strategy is to cut emissions by using cleaner energy sources and less energy overall. However, many experts argue that this alone will not be enough to meet international climate targets. As a result, increasing attention is being paid to carbon capture technology, which aims to trap carbon dioxide before it enters the atmosphere, or even to remove it from the air after it has been released.","The most established form of the technology is known as carbon capture and storage, or CCS. It is usually applied at large industrial sites, such as power stations, cement factories and steel works, which release huge amounts of carbon dioxide. Special chemical solvents separate carbon dioxide from the other waste gases. The gas is then compressed, transported through pipelines and injected deep underground into rock formations, such as old oil and gas fields, where it can be stored for thousands of years. In some cases, the captured gas is used instead to manufacture other products, a process known as carbon capture and utilization.","A newer approach, direct air capture, removes carbon dioxide directly from the surrounding air. Large fans draw air through filters containing chemicals that bind with the gas. Because the concentration of carbon dioxide in ordinary air is very low, about 0.04 per cent, this process requires a great deal of energy and is currently much more expensive than capturing gas from factory chimneys. Nevertheless, it has one important advantage: it can deal with emissions from scattered sources, such as cars and aircraft, which cannot easily be captured at the point where they are produced.","Saudi Arabia is among the countries investing in this field. The Kingdom has promoted the idea of a 'circular carbon economy', in which carbon is reduced, reused, recycled and removed. At Hawiyah, a facility operated by Saudi Aramco captures carbon dioxide from a gas plant and injects it into an oil field, and the country has announced plans for a major carbon capture hub in Jubail, one of its largest industrial cities.","Critics, however, raise several concerns. First, the technology remains costly, and capturing carbon consumes a significant share of the energy produced by a power station. Second, the stored gas must be monitored carefully to ensure that it does not leak. Third, and perhaps most importantly, some environmental groups fear that carbon capture could become an excuse for delaying the move away from fossil fuels. In fact, much of the carbon dioxide captured so far has been used to push more oil out of the ground, which may increase total emissions rather than reduce them.","Supporters respond that, for certain industries, there is currently no realistic alternative. Cement production, for example, releases carbon dioxide through chemical reactions, not only through burning fuel, so even a factory powered entirely by renewable energy would still produce emissions. Most climate scientists now agree that carbon capture can play a useful role, but only alongside, and not instead of, rapid cuts in emissions. Whether the technology can be scaled up quickly and cheaply enough to make a real difference remains one of the key questions of the coming decades."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the methods, benefits and limits of capturing carbon dioxide","why cement factories should close","the history of oil in Saudi Arabia","how air pollution affects human health"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage describes the technology and the debate.","h":"طرق احتجاز الكربون وحدودها."},{"n":22,"s":"In carbon capture and storage, where is the gas usually kept?","o":["in large tanks at the factories","deep underground in rock formations","in containers on the seabed","in the upper atmosphere"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"تحت الأرض."},{"n":23,"s":"Why is direct air capture so expensive?","o":["The fans must be imported.","The filters must be replaced every day.","Governments tax it heavily.","Carbon dioxide in ordinary air is very dilute, so much energy is needed."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"التركيز منخفض فيحتاج طاقة كبيرة."},{"n":24,"s":"The word 'scattered' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["very large","spread over a wide area","extremely dirty","owned by the government"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Cars and aircraft are spread out everywhere.","h":"متفرقة."},{"n":25,"s":"What is the main advantage of direct air capture?","o":["It is cheaper than other methods.","It needs no energy at all.","It can deal with emissions from cars and aircraft.","It produces more oil."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 states its advantage.","h":"يعالج الانبعاثات المتفرقة."},{"n":26,"s":"What does the 'circular carbon economy' involve?","o":["banning all fossil fuels immediately","exporting carbon dioxide to other countries","relying only on planting trees","reducing, reusing, recycling and removing carbon"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 defines it.","h":"الاقتصاد الدائري للكربون."},{"n":27,"s":"Which of the following is NOT a concern raised by critics?","o":["The technology is costly.","Stored gas might leak.","It may delay the move away from fossil fuels.","The technology creates too many jobs."],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Jobs are not mentioned.","h":"الوظائف غير مذكورة."},{"n":28,"s":"What can be inferred about using captured carbon dioxide to extract more oil?","o":["It is the best use of the technology.","It has been banned in most countries.","It may not reduce overall emissions.","It is supported by all environmental groups."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"It 'may increase total emissions'.","h":"قد لا يقلل الانبعاثات."},{"n":29,"s":"Why does the writer mention cement production?","o":["to give an example of emissions that renewable energy alone cannot prevent","to show that cement is no longer needed","to compare the prices of building materials","to criticize factory workers"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"Cement releases CO2 through chemical reactions.","h":"مثال على انبعاثات لا تمنعها الطاقة المتجددة."},{"n":30,"s":"How would you describe the writer's overall approach?","o":["completely opposed to the technology","enthusiastic without any reservations","balanced, presenting both benefits and concerns","uninterested in the topic"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"Both sides are presented.","h":"متوازن."}]},{"t":"The sharing economy","p":["In the past, if people needed a ride across town, a place to stay on holiday or a drill to put up a shelf, they usually called a taxi, booked a hotel or bought a drill. Today, many of them turn instead to digital platforms that connect them with ordinary individuals who are willing to share what they own. This model, often called the 'sharing economy', has grown rapidly since the late 2000s and now includes ride-hailing apps, home-sharing websites, car-sharing schemes and platforms for renting tools, clothes and even parking spaces.","The basic idea is not new. Neighbours have always borrowed from one another, and families have long shared cars and homes. What has changed is technology. Smartphones, GPS, online payment systems and rating systems have made it possible for strangers to trust one another and to complete transactions quickly and safely. When a user can see that a driver has received hundreds of positive reviews, they are more likely to get into the car. In Saudi Arabia, for example, ride-hailing apps have become a common way to travel in large cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah, and many citizens now work as part-time drivers.","Supporters argue that the sharing economy offers several benefits. For consumers, it often means lower prices and more choice. For providers, it creates opportunities to earn extra income from assets that would otherwise remain unused; the average privately owned car, for example, is parked for more than ninety per cent of the time. There may also be environmental advantages: if people share cars and tools rather than buying their own, fewer products need to be manufactured, which saves resources and energy.","However, the sharing economy has also generated considerable controversy. Traditional businesses, such as taxi companies and hotels, complain that platform providers compete unfairly, because they often avoid the licences, safety inspections and taxes that established businesses must pay. In several cities, the growth of short-term home rentals has been blamed for reducing the number of homes available to local residents and pushing up rents, leading some authorities to limit the number of nights a property can be rented.","Another major debate concerns the people who provide the services. Most platforms treat drivers and other workers as independent contractors rather than employees. This gives workers flexibility to choose when and how much they work, which many value. However, it also means that they may not receive benefits such as sick pay, holidays or a guaranteed minimum income. Courts in several countries have been asked to decide whether these workers should have the same rights as ordinary employees, and the results have varied.","Some observers also question whether the term 'sharing' is accurate at all. When a person rents out an apartment all year round to tourists, or drives for a ride-hailing company full-time, the activity looks less like sharing and more like a conventional business. For this reason, some researchers prefer terms such as the 'platform economy' or the 'access economy'. Whatever it is called, this way of doing business is likely to remain, and the challenge for governments is to develop regulations that protect workers, consumers and communities without destroying the convenience and innovation that have made these services so popular."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how to become a taxi driver","the growth, benefits and problems of the sharing economy","the history of hotels","why people should buy their own tools"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage covers all these aspects.","h":"اقتصاد المشاركة."},{"n":32,"s":"According to the passage, what has made the sharing economy possible?","o":["lower taxes on small businesses","laws that require people to share","a fall in the number of cars","new technology such as smartphones and online payment"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2: 'What has changed is technology.'","h":"التقنية الحديثة."},{"n":33,"s":"Why does the writer mention that cars are parked for over ninety per cent of the time?","o":["to argue that cars are too expensive","to criticize car owners","to explain traffic jams","to show that many assets are underused"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"It supports the idea of unused assets.","h":"الأصول غير المستغلة."},{"n":34,"s":"The word 'controversy' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["financial success","government support","public disagreement","technical difficulty"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The model has caused strong disagreement.","h":"جدل."},{"n":35,"s":"Why do taxi companies and hotels say the competition is unfair?","o":["Platform providers often avoid licences, inspections and taxes.","Platform drivers are better trained.","Platforms receive government money.","Taxis are not allowed to use apps."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"تهرب من التراخيص والضرائب."},{"n":36,"s":"According to the passage, what has happened in some cities because of short-term rentals?","o":["Hotels have become much cheaper.","Tourism has stopped completely.","Taxi companies have closed.","Fewer homes are available to residents and rents have risen."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 states this.","h":"قلة المساكن وارتفاع الإيجار."},{"n":37,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'This' in 'This gives workers flexibility' refers to","o":["treating workers as independent contractors","receiving sick pay","going to court","choosing a platform"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"It refers to the previous sentence.","h":"معاملة العمال كمستقلين."},{"n":38,"s":"What can be inferred from the statement 'the results have varied'?","o":["All courts have agreed with the platforms.","Courts in different countries have reached different decisions.","Workers have won every case.","No court has yet made a decision."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Varied results mean different decisions.","h":"أحكام مختلفة."},{"n":39,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of the sharing economy?","o":["a guaranteed minimum income for workers","lower prices for consumers","extra income for providers","savings in resources and energy"],"k":0,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"The lack of a guaranteed income is a disadvantage.","h":"الدخل المضمون ليس ميزة."},{"n":40,"s":"What is the main purpose of the final paragraph?","o":["to explain how to rent an apartment","to list examples of new apps","to question the name 'sharing' and consider the future","to compare taxi prices"],"k":2,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"It discusses the term and the challenge ahead.","h":"مناقشة التسمية والمستقبل."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Not until the results were published ______ how serious the problem was.","o":["we realized","did we realize","we did realize","had we realize"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did we realize — Not until … at the start: inversion with did.","h":"القلب بعد Not until."},{"n":42,"s":"If she ______ the scholarship last year, she would be studying in Canada now.","o":["won","would win","has won","had won"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had won — Mixed conditional: past condition, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":43,"s":"______ by the sudden noise, the baby started to cry.","o":["Frightening","To frighten","Having frightening","Frightened"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Frightened — Past participle clause with passive meaning.","h":"اسم المفعول."},{"n":44,"s":"It is essential that every applicant ______ the form before Friday.","o":["submitting","submit","to submit","will submitted"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"submit — Subjunctive after 'It is essential that'.","h":"صيغة الطلب."},{"n":45,"s":"The new stadium is said ______ more than two billion riyals.","o":["to cost having","having cost","to have cost","that it cost"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to have cost — Passive reporting about the past: to have + past participle.","h":"is said to have."},{"n":46,"s":"I wish I ______ more attention in the chemistry lessons last year.","o":["had paid","paid","would pay","have paid"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had paid — Wish about the past: past perfect.","h":"wish مع الماضي التام."},{"n":47,"s":"What surprised me most ______ how quickly the children learned the rules.","o":["were","was","it was","being"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was — Cleft sentence with a singular verb.","h":"جملة التوكيد."},{"n":48,"s":"Hardly ______ the lecture begun when the fire alarm rang.","o":["had","did","has","was"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had — Hardly + had + subject + past participle.","h":"Hardly had."},{"n":49,"s":"The scientists, most of ______ had worked on the project for years, were delighted.","o":["who","whom","them","which"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whom — most of + whom for people.","h":"most of whom."},{"n":50,"s":"______ he been more careful, the accident would never have happened.","o":["If","Were","Had","Should"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Had — Inverted third conditional.","h":"Had بدل If."},{"n":51,"s":"The engineers ______ on the design for six months when the funding was suddenly withdrawn.","o":["had been working","have been working","were worked","are working"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had been working — Past perfect continuous for duration before a past event.","h":"الماضي التام المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"She firmly denied ______ the confidential documents to the press.","o":["to leak","to have leaking","having leaked","leak"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"having leaked — deny + -ing (perfect gerund).","h":"deny + -ing."},{"n":53,"s":"The manager insisted ______ the report again before sending it.","o":["to check","on check","for checking","on checking"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"on checking — insist on + -ing.","h":"insist on."},{"n":54,"s":"______ the weather improves, the final match will be cancelled.","o":["Provided","As long as","Unless","If"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Unless — Unless = if not.","h":"Unless."},{"n":55,"s":"It's high time the city council ______ something about the traffic.","o":["did","does","will do","has done"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did — It's high time + past simple.","h":"It's high time."},{"n":56,"s":"All the windows ______ before the storm arrives this evening.","o":["should close","should be closed","should be closing","should have close"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"should be closed — Modal passive.","h":"المبني للمجهول مع should."},{"n":57,"s":"______ his lack of experience, he was offered the position.","o":["Although","However","Even if","Despite"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Despite — Despite + noun phrase.","h":"Despite."},{"n":58,"s":"Our professor advised us ______ our sources carefully.","o":["to check","checking","that check","check"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to check — advise + object + to-infinitive.","h":"advise sb to."},{"n":59,"s":"The more you practise, ______ you will become.","o":["more confident","the most confident","the confident","the more confident"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the more confident — Double comparative.","h":"the more … the more."},{"n":60,"s":"Personally, I would rather ______ at home than go to the crowded mall.","o":["staying","to stay","stay","stayed"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"stay — would rather + base verb.","h":"would rather + المصدر."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The number of students (A) who (B) applied for the course (C) have increased (D) sharply.","o":["who","applied","have","sharply"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"have — The number … takes a singular verb: has.","h":"The number مفرد."},{"n":62,"s":"(A) Despite of the heavy rain, the (B) outdoor concert (C) went (D) ahead.","o":["Despite of","outdoor","went","ahead"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Despite of — Despite takes no 'of'.","h":"Despite بدون of."},{"n":63,"s":"Not only (A) did the team (B) win the final, but they (C) also (D) breaking the record.","o":["did","win","also","breaking"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"breaking — Past simple needed: broke.","h":"broke."},{"n":64,"s":"If I (A) had known you were coming, I (B) would prepare a (C) special (D) meal.","o":["had known","would prepare","special","meal"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"would prepare — Third conditional: would have prepared.","h":"would have prepared."},{"n":65,"s":"The research, (A) which was (B) carried out over five years, (C) show (D) clearly that sleep affects memory.","o":["which","carried out","show","clearly"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"show — The research is singular: shows.","h":"الفاعل مفرد."},{"n":66,"s":"(A) Having finishing the report, she (B) sent it (C) to her (D) manager.","o":["Having finishing","sent","to","manager"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Having finishing — Perfect participle: Having finished.","h":"Having finished."},{"n":67,"s":"It was (A) such a (B) difficult question that (C) nobody (D) could answered it.","o":["such","difficult","nobody","could answered"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"could answered — could + base verb: could answer.","h":"could + المصدر."},{"n":68,"s":"Hardly (A) had we arrived (B) than it (C) started to (D) rain.","o":["had","than","started","rain"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"than — Hardly … when.","h":"Hardly … when."},{"n":69,"s":"(A) The most of the (B) participants (C) found the workshop (D) useful.","o":["The most","participants","found","useful"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"The most — Most of the … (no 'The').","h":"Most of بدون The."},{"n":70,"s":"I look forward (A) to (B) hearing from you and (C) to discuss the project (D) further.","o":["to","hearing","to discuss","further"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"to discuss — Parallel structure: to discussing.","h":"to discussing."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M21-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"Why is the customer unhappy?","o":["The oil change was not done.","His car is still not ready.","The brakes were not repaired.","The garage did extra work without asking him."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"'nobody called me to ask'.","h":"عمل إضافي دون إذن."}],"t":"An unexpected bill from a mechanic","sc":["Situation: A customer speaks to a mechanic at a garage. (88 words)","Man: Excuse me, I brought my car in for an oil change this morning, but this bill is for over a thousand riyals. An oil change should cost about two hundred.","Man: Yes, sir, but when we checked the car, we found that the front brake pads were badly worn, so we replaced them.","Man: But nobody called me to ask. I'd probably have agreed, but I expect to be consulted first.","Man: You're right, we should have called. I'll speak to the manager about giving you a discount on the labour."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M21-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What condition does the supervisor set?","o":["She must work extra hours after the wedding.","She must find someone to replace her.","The employee must finish her audit work by the twentieth.","She must take only one day off."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'If you can have everything ready by the twentieth'.","h":"إنهاء العمل قبل العشرين."}],"t":"Asking a supervisor for leave","sc":["Situation: An employee speaks to her supervisor. (81 words)","Woman: Ms. Reem, I was wondering whether I could take three days' leave at the end of the month. My sister is getting married in Abha.","Woman: Congratulations! The only problem is that the annual audit is scheduled for that week, and I need the whole team here.","Woman: I understand. What if I finished my part of the audit preparation before I leave and stayed available by phone?","Woman: If you can have everything ready by the twentieth, then I don't see a problem."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M21-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"Why is the information different?","o":["The train has been cancelled.","The screen is broken.","The train leaves from another station.","The man was reading an out-of-date timetable."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"The wall shows the winter schedule.","h":"جدول قديم."}],"t":"A confusing train timetable","sc":["Situation: A passenger speaks to a station employee. (78 words)","Man: According to this timetable, the next train to Riyadh leaves at three forty. But the screen says four fifteen.","Woman: The timetable on the wall is the winter schedule. The summer timetable started last week, so the screen is correct.","Man: So I've got almost an hour to wait. Is there a café in the station?","Woman: Yes, on the upper level, next to the ticket office. Just make sure you're back at platform two by four o'clock, when boarding begins."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M21-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What will the man do?","o":["write a completely new article","phone the desalination plant","apologize to readers on the radio","have the article corrected and add a note"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'correct it immediately and add a note'.","h":"تصحيح المقال وإضافة ملاحظة."}],"t":"A typo in a published article","sc":["Situation: Two journalists are talking in an office. (70 words)","Woman: Have you seen this morning's article on the new desalination plant? It says the plant will produce sixty thousand cubic metres a day.","Man: Yes, I wrote it. Is something wrong?","Woman: The press release said six hundred thousand. You've missed a zero, and that changes the whole story.","Man: Oh no, you're right. I'll ask the website team to correct it immediately and add a note at the end explaining the correction."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M21-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What is the second man doing?","o":["accepting an invitation","declining an invitation and giving a reason","making a complaint","asking for directions"],"k":1,"ty":"function","e":"He says 'I'd love to, but …'.","h":"اعتذار مهذب مع سبب."}],"t":"A friend declines an invitation politely","sc":["Situation: Two friends are talking after class. (66 words)","Man: Some of us are going camping in the desert this weekend. Would you like to join us?","Man: That's really kind of you, and I'd love to, but I promised my grandfather I'd help him move to his new flat on Friday and Saturday.","Man: That's a pity. Maybe next time, then.","Man: Definitely. Please let me know when you're planning the next trip, and send me some photos!"]},{"rec":6,"a":"M21-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"How will the lecturer first give feedback on the essay plan?","o":["at the conference","in a meeting on Tuesday","by email","through another lecturer"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'll reply with comments by Wednesday morning'.","h":"بالبريد الإلكتروني."}],"t":"A lecturer cancels office hours","sc":["Situation: A lecturer speaks to a student. (67 words)","Woman: I'm afraid I have to cancel my office hours this Tuesday. I've been asked to attend a conference in Dammam.","Man: I was hoping to discuss my essay plan with you before the deadline.","Woman: Send it to me by email tonight, and I'll reply with comments by Wednesday morning. If you still have questions, we can talk on the phone on Thursday.","Man: That works. Thank you, Dr. Lina."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M21-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"Why does the man agree to take the later flight?","o":["He wants to stay in a hotel.","He does not need to arrive until Thursday.","His flight was cancelled.","He has already missed his flight."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'My meeting isn't until Thursday afternoon'.","h":"اجتماعه يوم الخميس."}],"t":"An overbooked flight","sc":["Situation: An airline employee speaks to a passenger at the gate. (63 words)","Woman: Good evening. I'm afraid this flight is overbooked. We're looking for volunteers to take the next flight, which leaves tomorrow morning at seven.","Man: What would I get if I agreed to wait?","Woman: We'd provide a hotel room near the airport, meals and a travel voucher worth fifteen hundred riyals.","Man: My meeting isn't until Thursday afternoon, so that's fine. I'll take the later flight."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M21-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What is the second woman's opinion of the Italian restaurant?","o":["The pasta is not good.","It is too expensive.","It is too crowded.","It is too far away. Longer conversations"],"k":2,"ty":"opinion","e":"'It's always crowded'.","h":"مزدحم جدًا."}],"t":"Disagreeing about a restaurant choice","sc":["Situation: Two friends are deciding where to eat. (71 words)","Woman: Let's try the new Italian restaurant on Prince Sultan Road. Everyone says the pasta is excellent.","Woman: I'd rather not. It's always crowded, and you have to wait at least forty minutes for a table.","Woman: We could book in advance.","Woman: They don't take bookings. Why don't we go to the Lebanese place near the corniche instead? It's quieter, and we can sit outside.","Woman: Fine, but next time we're trying the Italian one."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M21-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"What is the supervisor's main criticism of the proposal?","o":["The topic is too broad.","The topic is not important.","The questionnaire is too long.","The proposal is too short."],"k":0,"ty":"opinion","e":"'the scope is too wide'.","h":"الموضوع واسع جدًا."},{"n":80,"s":"Why does the supervisor suggest using sleep diaries?","o":["Diaries are cheaper than questionnaires.","People often report their sleep inaccurately in questionnaires.","The ethics committee requires them.","Parents prefer diaries."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"He says self-reports are inaccurate.","h":"دقة أكبر."}],"t":"Discussing a thesis proposal","sc":["Situation: A student meets her supervisor. (195 words)","Woman: Dr. Saleh, thank you for meeting me. I've finished the draft of my thesis proposal on the effects of social media on teenagers' sleep.","Man: I've read it. The topic is interesting and important, but I think the scope is too wide. 'Social media' and 'teenagers' cover a huge range.","Woman: What would you suggest?","Man: Focus on a specific group, for example secondary school students in Taif, and on one or two platforms. That will make your data collection manageable.","Woman: That makes sense. I was planning to use a questionnaire. Is that enough?","Man: Questionnaires are useful, but people often report their sleep inaccurately. You could ask a smaller group to keep sleep diaries for two weeks as well. Combining the two methods would make your findings more reliable.","Woman: Would I need ethical approval for that?","Man: Yes. Because your participants are under eighteen, you'll need permission from the university's ethics committee and consent from their parents. Apply early, because the committee only meets once a month.","Woman: I'll revise the proposal this week and send it to you by Sunday.","Man: Good. Also add more recent studies to your literature review. Most of your sources are over ten years old."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M21-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"What will the second man be responsible for?","o":["the introduction and conclusion","the layout and the graphs","printing the poster","booking a room in the library"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'I could do the layout and the graphs'.","h":"التصميم والرسوم."},{"n":82,"s":"Why do they plan to practise in front of Dr. Amal?","o":["to get feedback before the poster is printed","to ask her to judge the competition","to show her the shared folder","to ask for more time"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"'so she can give us feedback before the final printing'.","h":"للحصول على ملاحظات."}],"t":"Planning a joint research poster","sc":["Situation: Two students are preparing for a conference. (182 words)","Man: The poster deadline for the research conference is next Thursday. We need to decide how to divide the work.","Man: I think the biggest challenge is space. We have to present a whole year's research on air quality in Riyadh on a single poster.","Man: Exactly. The organizers recommend no more than eight hundred words. So we should focus on our main findings and use graphs rather than long paragraphs.","Man: I'm better with the design software, so I could do the layout and the graphs. Could you write the text?","Man: Sure. I'll write the introduction, methods and conclusion, and keep each section short. What about the results?","Man: Let's write that section together, since it's the heart of the poster. We can meet on Sunday afternoon in the library.","Man: Good. And we should prepare a two-minute explanation for visitors. At the last conference, the judges asked every group to summarize their poster.","Man: Right. Let's practise it on Tuesday in front of Dr. Amal, so she can give us feedback before the final printing on Wednesday.","Man: Perfect. I'll create a shared folder for our files tonight."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M21-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Why does Maha want to discuss the workload?","o":["She is too busy with exams.","She did most of the work on the last project.","She wants to change groups.","The lecturer asked them to."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'Last time, I ended up writing almost the whole report.'","h":"قامت بمعظم العمل سابقًا."},{"n":84,"s":"How will the group give the presentation?","o":["Maha will present alone.","They will record a video.","Each member will present her own part.","The lecturer will choose one speaker. Talks and lectures"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'Why don't we all present the part we worked on?'","h":"كل واحدة تعرض جزءها."}],"t":"Negotiating a group workload","sc":["Situation: Three students are planning a group project. (194 words)","Maha: Before we start, I think we need to talk about how we're dividing the work on this marketing project. Last time, I ended up writing almost the whole report.","Ruba: That's fair. I was very busy with exams last time, but I can do more now.","Joud: Me too. Let's make a list of all the tasks first: the market research, the survey, the analysis, the report and the presentation.","Maha: The survey and the analysis are probably the heaviest parts. Maybe one person could design and distribute the survey, and another could analyse the results.","Ruba: I'll design the survey. I've used online survey tools before.","Joud: Then I'll do the analysis. I'm comfortable with spreadsheets.","Maha: In that case, I'll do the market research and write the first draft of the report. But I'd like both of you to write the sections on your own parts.","Ruba: That's reasonable. And the presentation?","Joud: Why don't we all present the part we worked on? That way, the lecturer can see everyone's contribution.","Maha: Good idea. Let's also set internal deadlines. The survey should go out by the fifteenth, so there's time for analysis.","Ruba: Agreed. I'll put the deadlines in our group calendar."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M21-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"According to the lecturer, what is the main problem with very low water prices?","o":["People have little reason to save water.","Desalination plants have to close.","The quality of water becomes poor.","Governments earn too much money."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'When water is cheap, people have little reason to save it.'","h":"لا دافع للتوفير."},{"n":86,"s":"How do increasing block tariffs work?","o":["Each additional amount of water costs more than the previous one.","Every household pays the same fixed amount.","Water is free for large families.","Prices change according to the season."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'Each additional block becomes more expensive.'","h":"شرائح متصاعدة."},{"n":87,"s":"Why does the lecturer mention smart meters?","o":["They detect leaks in public pipes.","They replace the need for tariffs.","They make water cheaper for everyone.","They help households see their usage and reduce consumption."],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"They show usage in real time.","h":"تساعد على خفض الاستهلاك."}],"t":"Extended lecture: the economics of water pricing","sc":["Situation: An economics lecturer speaks to students. (316 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today's lecture deals with a question that is especially important in dry countries: how should water be priced? Water is essential for life, so many people feel that it should be free or very cheap. Indeed, in many countries, governments have traditionally kept water prices far below the real cost of supplying it. In the Gulf region, for instance, much drinking water comes from desalination, which requires large amounts of energy, yet households have often paid only a small fraction of the production cost. Economists point out that low prices create a problem. When water is cheap, people have little reason to save it. They may water gardens in the middle of the day, when much of the water evaporates, or ignore leaking pipes. As a result, consumption per person in some low-price countries is among the highest in the world, even where water is scarce. One solution is to raise prices so that they reflect the true cost. However, this raises a question of fairness: poorer families could struggle to pay for a basic necessity. To address this, many cities use a system called increasing block tariffs. Under this system, the first block of water, enough for essential needs such as drinking, cooking and washing, is sold at a low price. Each additional block becomes more expensive. This means that households pay little for what they need, but those who use large amounts, for filling swimming pools, for example, pay much more. Research suggests that such tariffs can reduce consumption significantly, especially when combined with smart meters that show households how much they are using in real time. Of course, pricing is only one tool. Reducing leaks in distribution networks, recycling wastewater for agriculture and industry, and educating the public are also essential. In our next lecture, we'll examine agricultural water use, which accounts for the largest share of consumption in most countries."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M21-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"How are glaciers formed?","o":["More snow falls in winter than melts in summer.","Sea water freezes during the winter.","Rain freezes on the tops of mountains.","Ice moves slowly from the poles."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated at the start of the lecture.","h":"تراكم الثلج."},{"n":89,"s":"What do the air bubbles in ice cores show?","o":["the number of volcanic eruptions","the exact age of the glacier","the amount of certain gases in the ancient atmosphere","the speed of ancient winds"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"They show carbon dioxide and methane levels.","h":"مكونات الغلاف الجوي القديم."},{"n":90,"s":"Why are ice cores being stored in Antarctica?","o":["to make them easier to drill","to count the years more accurately","to keep them away from scientists","to preserve them before the glaciers melt"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Melting glaciers lose their records.","h":"للحفاظ عليها."}],"t":"Lecture: how glaciers record past climates","sc":["Situation: A geography lecturer speaks to students. (317 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. In today's session, we'll look at how scientists use glaciers and ice sheets to learn about the Earth's climate in the past. Glaciers form in places where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer. Year after year, the snow builds up, and the weight of new layers compresses the older snow beneath into solid ice. In very cold regions such as Antarctica and Greenland, this process has continued for hundreds of thousands of years, creating ice sheets that are more than three kilometres thick. To study this ice, scientists drill deep into it and remove long cylinders called ice cores. Each core is like a history book, with the most recent snow at the top and the oldest at the bottom. In some cores, individual years can be counted, rather like the rings of a tree. So what can the ice tell us? First, when snow falls, it traps tiny bubbles of air. These bubbles are samples of the ancient atmosphere, so by analysing them, scientists can measure how much carbon dioxide and methane the air contained at different times. Second, the chemical composition of the ice itself, in particular the ratio of different forms of oxygen, reveals the temperature at the time the snow fell. Third, layers of dust and volcanic ash record events such as major eruptions and periods of strong winds. The oldest continuous ice core, drilled in Antarctica, covers about eight hundred thousand years. It shows a clear pattern: whenever carbon dioxide levels were high, temperatures were also high. It also shows that today's carbon dioxide levels are far higher than at any point in that entire period. Unfortunately, these records are themselves under threat. As glaciers melt, the information they contain is lost. For this reason, an international project is now storing ice cores from mountain glaciers in a special freezer in Antarctica, preserving them for future scientists."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Learning to cook is a valuable life skill for young people. (2) It allows them to prepare healthy meals instead of relying on fast food. (3) It can also save a great deal of money over time. (4) Many famous chefs have their own television programmes. (5) Moreover, cooking for others can be a way of showing care and hospitality."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"Famous chefs on television do not develop the point about cooking as a life skill.","h":"الطهاة المشاهير خارج الفكرة."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["With clear rules on speed and parking, scooters could become a safe and useful form of transport.","Scooters were invented many years ago.","Some people prefer to walk to work.","Batteries can be recycled."],"x":["Electric scooters have become popular in many cities. They are cheap to rent, easy to use and produce no emissions while running. However, they have also caused accidents, and scooters left on pavements can block pedestrians. ______"],"k":0,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"The conclusion balances the benefits and problems.","h":"خلاصة متوازنة."},{"n":93,"s":"The results (A) collected during the experiment (B) were analysed (C) careful by (D) the research team.","o":["collected","were","careful","the"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"An adverb is needed to describe the verb: carefully.","h":"الظرف carefully."},{"n":94,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["3 – 2 – 4 – 1","2 – 4 – 3 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1"],"x":["(1) As a result, the city now has one of the lowest accident rates in the region.","(2) Ten years ago, the city had a serious problem with road accidents.","(3) The authorities introduced speed cameras and stricter fines.","(4) They also redesigned dangerous junctions and improved street lighting."],"k":3,"ty":"best order","e":"problem → actions → more actions → result.","h":"المشكلة ثم الحلول ثم النتيجة."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Volunteering offers many benefits to young people. (2) It helps them develop skills such as teamwork and leadership. (3) It allows them to meet people from different backgrounds. (4) It can also strengthen university applications. (5) Some universities are located in very large cities."],"k":3,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"University locations do not develop the point about volunteering.","h":"موقع الجامعات خارج الفكرة."},{"n":96,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Students do not all learn in the same way.","Teachers work very long hours.","Reading is an important skill.","Practical activities can be expensive."],"x":["______ Some students learn best by listening, others by reading, and others by doing practical activities. Effective teachers therefore use a variety of methods to reach every learner."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details describe different ways of learning.","h":"اختلاف أساليب التعلم."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Many novels are very long.","In this way, stories can help us understand and connect with other people.","Libraries lend books for free.","Some films are based on novels."],"x":["Reading fiction does more than entertain us. When we follow a character's thoughts and feelings, we practise seeing the world from another person's point of view. Studies suggest that regular readers of fiction score higher on tests of empathy. ______"],"k":1,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It sums up the effect on empathy.","h":"خلاصة الفكرة."},{"n":98,"s":"Neither the teacher (A) nor the students (B) was aware (C) that the exam (D) had been moved.","o":["nor","was","that","had been"],"k":1,"ty":"error","e":"With neither … nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject: were.","h":"الفعل يتبع الأقرب: were."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Offices are usually open from eight to four.","Some companies have offices in several countries.","Computers have become much faster.","This saves commuting time and often increases employees' satisfaction."],"x":["Many companies now allow employees to work from home for part of the week. ______ On the other hand, some workers feel isolated and find it difficult to separate work from family life."],"k":3,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"An advantage is needed before 'On the other hand'.","h":"ميزة قبل On the other hand."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Therefore","However","Similarly","For example Teacher Section — Mock 21"],"x":["The new medicine proved highly effective in laboratory tests. ______, it caused serious side effects in some patients during clinical trials."],"k":1,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"The second sentence contrasts with the first.","h":"However للتضاد."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
